## Why This Is Asked
This tests fixed-length path DP and path reconstruction in graphs, a core DP pattern that shows depth beyond standard knapsack.

## Key Concepts
- DP on path length t
- State transition using edges
- Backpointer reconstruction
- Time and space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEdgePath(n, edges, k) {
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) dp[v][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (const e of edges) {
      const {u,v,w} = e;
      const prev = dp[u][t-1];
      if (prev !== NEG) {
        const cand = prev + w;
        if (cand > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = cand;
          par[v][t] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let bestV = 0, best = NEG;
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) if (dp[v][k] > best) { best = dp[v][k]; bestV = v; }
  const path = [];
  let cur = bestV;
  for (let t=k; t>=1; t--) { path.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; }
  path.push(cur);
  path.reverse();
  return {path, weight: best};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this change if edges could have negative weights? How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a DP with two choices per step (1- or 2-length blocks), plus reconstruction and complexity awareness.

## Key Concepts

- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Transitions for 1-length and 2-length blocks
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      // 1-length block ending at i-1
      if(dp[i-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 1; }
      }
      // 2-length block ending at i-1
      if(i>=2 && dp[i-2][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-2][t-1] + A[i-2] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 2; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back from (n, k)
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

